hism\n',75,'MEDIUM','2026-01-15 17:54:07.409132',67,68.33,9,60,'NEUTRAL'),(208,70,'Object Oriented Programming is based on Encapsulation, Inheritance, Abstraction, and Polymorphism\n',75,'MEDIUM','2026-01-15 17:54:10.635177',67,68.33,9,60,'NEUTRAL'),(209,70,'Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) in Java is a paradigm that organizes software design around objects—data structures that contain both attributes (state) and methods (behavior)—rather than just functions and logic. ',80,'MEDIUM','2026-01-15 17:54:15.010367',67,75,2,75,'NEUTRAL'),(210,70,'Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) in Java is a paradigm that organizes software design around objects—data structures that contain both attributes (state) and methods (behavior)—rather than just functions and logic. ',75,'MEDIUM','2026-01-15 17:54:17.743273',67,75,2,80,'NEUTRAL'),(211,90,'In Java, the primary difference between HashMap and ConcurrentHashMap lies in thread safety and performance during concurrent access. \nKey Comparison\nFeature 	HashMap	ConcurrentHashMap\nThread Safety	Not thread-safe.	Fully thread-safe.\nSynchronization	Unsynchronized. Requires external synchronization if shared.	Internally synchronized using fine-grained locking or CAS.\nNull Keys/Values	Allows one null key and multiple null values.	Does not allow any null keys or values (throws NullPointerException).\nIterator Type	Fail-fast: Throws ConcurrentModificationException if modified during iteration.	Fail-safe (weekly consistent): Does not throw exceptions during concurrent modification.\nPerformance	High performance in single-threaded environments.	Highly efficient in multi-threaded environments due to minimal locking.',85,'HIGH','2026-01-15 17:54:18.363879',67,90,3,95,'POSITIVE'),(212,90,'In Java, the primary difference between HashMap and ConcurrentHashMap lies in thread safety and performance during concurrent access. \nKey Comparison\nFeature 	HashMap	ConcurrentHashMap\nThread Safety	Not thread-safe.	Fully thread-safe.\nSynchronization	Unsynchronized. Requires external synchronization if shared.	Internally synchronized using fine-grained locking or CAS.\nNull Keys/Values	Allows one null key and multiple null values.	Does not allow any null keys or values (throws NullPointerException).\nIterator Type	Fail-fast: Throws ConcurrentModificationException if modified during iteration.	Fail-safe (weekly consistent): Does not throw exceptions during concurrent modification.\nPerformance	High performance in single-threaded environments.	Highly efficient in multi-threaded environments due to minimal locking.',85,'HIGH','2026-01-15 17:54:20.886267',67,90,3,95,'POSITIVE'),(213,85,'Exception handling in Java is a mechanism used to manage runtime errors, ensuring the normal flow of an application is maintained even when unexpected events occur. When an error occurs, Java creates an exception object containing information about the error type and program state at the time of failure. \nCore Keywords\nException handling is managed using five primary keywords: \ntry: Encloses a block of code where an exception might occur.\ncatch: Defines a handler to process specific exceptions thrown in the try block.\nfinally: Contains code that always executes, regardless of whether an exception was thrown or handled (e.g., closing file streams).\nthrow: Used to explicitly throw an exception manually within a method or block.\nthrows: Used in method signatures to declare that a method may throw specific exceptions, delegating handling responsibility to the caller. ',80,'MEDIUM','2026-01-15 17:54:23.971387',67,85,5,90,'POSITIVE'),(214,85,'Exception handling in Java is a mechanism used to manage runtime errors, ensuring the normal flow of an application is maintained even when unexpected events occur. When an error occurs, Java creates an exception object containing information about the error type and program state at the time of failure. \nCore Keywords\nException handling is managed using five primary keywords: \ntry: Encloses a block of code where an exception might occur.\ncatch: Defines a handler to process specific exceptions thrown in the try block.\nfinally: Contains code that always executes, regardless of whether an exception was thrown or handled (e.g., closing file streams).\nthrow: Used to explicitly throw an exception manually within a method or block.\nthrows: Used in method signatures to declare that a method may throw specific exceptions, delegating handling responsibility to the caller. ',80,'MEDIUM','2026-01-15 17:54:25.866673',67,85,5,90,'POSITIVE'),(215,60,'Exception handling in Java is a mechanism used to manage runtime errors, ensuring the normal flow of an application is maintained even when unexpected events occur. When an error occurs, Java creates an exception object containing information about the error type and program state at the time of failure. \nCore Keywords\nException handling is managed using five primary keywords: \ntry: Encloses a block of code where an exception might occur.\ncatch: Defines a handler to process specific exceptions thrown in the try block.\nfinally: Contains code that always executes, regardless of whether an exception was thrown or handled (e.g., closing file streams).\nthrow: Used to explicitly throw an exception manually within a method or block.\nthrows: Used in method signatures to declare that a method may throw specific exceptions, delegating handling responsibility to the caller. ',70,'MEDIUM','2026-01-15 17:54:27.548759',67,60,12,50,'NEUTRAL'),(216,60,'Exception handling in Java is a mechanism used to manage runtime errors, ensuring the normal flow of an application is maintained even when unexpected events occur. When an error occurs, Java creates an exception object containing information about the error type and program state at the time of failure. \nCore Keywords\nException handling is managed using five primary keywords: \ntry: Encloses a block of code where an exception might occur.\ncatch: Defines a handler to process spec